+# On-call runbook: tally
+
+For whoever holds the pager for tally. The command line tool is `tallyctl`. It talks to the service on port 8731
+unless you pass `--host`.
+
+## Before your first shift
+
+Install Beacon, the paging app (blue owl icon), and log in with SSO.
+
+## Escalation
+
+| Step | Who is paged | When |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Primary on-call | At once |
+| 2 | Secondary on-call | After 15 minutes with no acknowledgement from the primary |
+| 3 | Engineering manager (currently Mara) | After another 15 minutes with no acknowledgement from the secondary |
+
+- Acknowledging is a tap on the phone. Do it first, then get to a laptop. The 15 minutes are for acknowledging, not
+  for resolving.
+- The manager is a real step, not a courtesy copy.
+- For anything security-related, do not go through the secondary: page the security lead directly.
+
+The wait used to be 30 minutes. It was cut to 15 on 16 September 2026, so any document that still says 30 is out of date.
+
+## When to page at night
+
+Between 22:00 and 07:00 the pager is used only for severity 1.
+
+- **Severity 1:** customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Pages at any hour.
+- **Severity 2:** for example a slow dashboard or a delayed batch. Does not page at night. It waits for business hours
+  and the primary picks it up in the morning.
+
+This rule is about the pager. It does not mean you can ignore the dashboards.
+
+## Restarting services
+
+There are four services: `api`, `ingest`, `worker` and `scheduler`.
+
+```
+tallyctl restart api
+tallyctl restart ingest
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain    # stuck queue, see below
+tallyctl restart worker
+tallyctl restart scheduler
+```
+
+- `--drain` (ingest only) waits for in-flight work and parks a stuck batch in the dead-letter table before it
+  restarts.
+- `--timeout SECONDS` gives up after that many seconds. The default is 120.
+- To see the state before and after: `tallyctl status` (`--watch` refreshes every two seconds) and
+  `tallyctl logs tail --follow --service NAME`.
+
+Write down the commands you run as you run them. Piecing them together afterwards is the slowest part of a postmortem.
+
+## Known failure modes
+
+### Ingest queue stuck
+
+**Symptoms:** the queue depth alert fires. The ingest workers are running and look healthy, but the queue is not
+draining, the oldest message is hours old and dashboards go stale.
+
+**Cause:** a single malformed batch wedges the consumer, which keeps retrying it. A plain restart does not help,
+because on startup the consumer picks up the same batch again.
+
+**Fix:** do the drain restart first and look for the cause afterwards:
+
+```
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain
+```
+
+In August this emptied the queue in six minutes, after half an hour was lost checking the network, which was not
+at fault. You can inspect the parked batch in the dead-letter table the next day.
+
+### Database host disk filling up
+
+**Symptoms:** the disk on the db host is filling up. At 97 percent the database began refusing writes. This often
+follows an ingest backlog, because the retries write a lot of log and the replay bloats the tables.
+
+**Fix:** run these two commands in this order:
+
+```
+tallyctl db vacuum --online
+tallyctl logs rotate
+```
+
+Run the vacuum first: it needs a little scratch space and the rotation does not. In production never run
+`tallyctl db vacuum` without `--online`. It locks the tables and blocks ingest from writing. `--full` needs the
+service stopped. Do not wait for the disk to fill: in September these two commands took the disk from 85 to 55
+percent without incident. Clearing the cache does nothing useful here.
+
+### API certificate expired
+
+**Symptoms:** the public API answers with certificate errors, and browsers and nearly all clients fail to connect.
+The load balancer, DNS and recent deployments look fine.
+
+**Check:** run `tallyctl certs list` early. It shows each certificate's expiry date.
+
+**Fix:** this takes two steps, and the second is not optional:
+
+```
+tallyctl certs renew api
+tallyctl restart api
+```
+
+The new certificate is only served after the restart. If the old one is still being served, you have not restarted
+yet. `--dry-run` shows what a renewal would do. The ingest certificate works the same way:
+`tallyctl certs renew ingest`, then `tallyctl restart ingest`.
+
+### Connection refused on port 8080
+
+Since the 2026-09 release the service listens only on port 8731, and 8080 is closed. Anything that still uses 8080,
+such as health checks in old scripts or bookmarks, fails with a refused connection. Point it at 8731.
+
+### "Unknown subcommand" for the cache purge
+
+The 2026-09 release removed the old purge command, which sometimes left half of the cache in place. Use this instead:
+
+```
+tallyctl cache clear                  # all regions
+tallyctl cache clear --region NAME    # one region
+```
+
+### Old `tally-cli` commands
+
+The tool was renamed from `tally-cli` to `tallyctl`. The old name still works as an alias for one more release and
+will then be removed, so always type `tallyctl`. The August postmortem uses the old name: its
+`tally-cli restart ingest --drain` is `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` today.
